WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
If a membrane protein opens a path, can it make a solute travel uphill?

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.180
The answer separates a doorway from a pump.

3
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.160
Follow one charged solute across this membrane.

4
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:18.767
In water, a charged solute wears a water shell.

5
00:00:20.027 --> 00:00:23.347
To enter the oily bilayer core, it must lose that help.

6
00:00:24.527 --> 00:00:26.587
The core offers almost nothing in return.

7
00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.627
This energy cost is the toll for leaving water.

8
00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:34.517
A channel offers a polar pathway through the oil.

9
00:00:35.797 --> 00:00:39.237
Its polar groups replace some help from water, lowering the toll.

10
00:00:40.757 --> 00:00:42.237
Solute now crosses much faster.

11
00:00:43.597 --> 00:00:46.697
Yet the open channel supplies no directional energy.

12
00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:51.407
Direction comes from the solute's electrochemical gradient.

13
00:00:52.847 --> 00:00:55.227
Concentration pushes from the crowded side.

14
00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:59.567
Voltage pulls opposite charges and pushes like charges.

15
00:01:01.007 --> 00:01:02.987
Together, those pushes define downhill.

16
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:09.150
Here, Na⁺ is more concentrated outside, while the cell interior is negative.

17
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.910
If a Na⁺ channel opens, which way will Na⁺ move?

18
00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.130
Follow both pushes before you answer.

19
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.000
Concentration pushes Na⁺ inward, away from the crowded outside.

20
00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.780
The negative interior also pulls positive Na⁺ inward.

21
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.860
Both pushes agree, so opening the channel speeds Na⁺ downhill into the cell.

22
00:01:38.883 --> 00:01:42.043
Now reverse the combined electrochemical gradient.

23
00:01:43.323 --> 00:01:46.843
The same open channel lets Na⁺ run the opposite way.

24
00:01:47.943 --> 00:01:51.023
The protein changes speed, not the downhill direction.

25
00:01:52.323 --> 00:01:56.943
A protein can change speed, but uphill transport requires energy coupling.

26
00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:01.927
Channels are pores, open to both sides when their gates open.

27
00:02:03.287 --> 00:02:07.867
Carriers instead bind cargo, change shape, and release it across the membrane.

28
00:02:09.287 --> 00:02:13.507
Either mechanism can move a solute downhill without supplying energy.

29
00:02:14.617 --> 00:02:18.317
A pump is a carrier coupled directly to an energy source.

30
00:02:19.577 --> 00:02:22.977
This view shows only Na⁺ from the Na⁺-K⁺ pump.

31
00:02:24.217 --> 00:02:28.937
ATP drives its shape change and Na⁺ uphill, charging the gradient battery.

32
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:34.730
In one animal-cell example, a coupled carrier spends that stored gradient.

33
00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.070
Na⁺ runs downhill through the carrier.

34
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.650
Glucose travels uphill in the same direction.

35
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:47.910
No ATP is spent there, but the Na⁺ gradient ultimately depends on the pump.

36
00:02:48.983 --> 00:02:51.423
Classify any crossing with three questions.

37
00:02:52.803 --> 00:02:55.043
Is the route a pore or alternating access?

38
00:02:56.303 --> 00:02:58.563
Is the solute moving downhill or uphill?

39
00:02:59.823 --> 00:03:04.143
If uphill, what supplies energy: ATP or another downhill flow?

40
00:03:05.183 --> 00:03:10.423
Opening a channel or cycling a passive carrier cannot push a solute against its

41
00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:15.383
gradient. Look for energy coupling before calling transport uphill.

42
00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:19.663
Only coupled energy can drive transport uphill.
